  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nPrimary item of interest continues to be the (murky) risk of severe \nstorms tonight and again on Tuesday. Water vapor, as of this midday \nwriting, depicts a sharp trough digging past the arrowhead of \nMinnesota. Well south of this, perhaps on the far leading edge of \nthe right-front exit region of a 50-70 knot 500mb jet, lies a \ncluster of thunderstorms that has persisted to some capacity \nthroughout the morning hours. The healthiest part of the line is\ndiving south of the Quad Cities posing no severe risk to our \nlocal area. However, lingering outflow boundaries or trailing \nMCVs appear to the focus for renewed, upstream convection early \nthis evening across Wisconsin and Iowa. \n\nGuidance this morning has been in fair agreement that showers \nor storms, likely sub- severe, move through after 11pm. Forecast\nsoundings depict rather dry, stable air below 600mb, as high \npressure over NYC continues to exert perhaps a hint of \ninfluence locally. Since yesterday, forecast bulk shear values \nnear 30 knots are favorable over previous, lesser values. Fading\ninstability remains troublesome, though mid-level lapse rates \nnear 7 C/km continue to offer a glimmer of hope that some \nconvection could sustain itself. Like yesterday, we overall \nremain doubtful of any significant convection this evening but \ncannot completely rule out rogue instances of damaging wind \ngusts or heavy rain. \n\nWith anticipated minimal severe weather concerns during the \novernight hours, attention then turns to the advertised cold front \nnow south of US 24 later Tuesday morning. This appears to be focus \nfor an isolated severe storm, but the upper-level support is \nlackluster as the trough has become positively tilted. Shear is \nnotably improved toward 40 knots but instability remains \nproblematic, especially considering any cloud debris from early-\nmorning showers/storms. Here too, not overly concerned about \nthe severe weather chances on Tuesday.\n\nBehind this cold front and period of showers and storms, a \nreinforcing dose of cool air descends along a trailing secondary \nupper-level vorticity maximum. This results in a 20-30% chance of \nshowers Tuesday night through Wednesday morning downwind of Lake \nMichigan amid 10C delta-Ts and cloud tops near 10,000 ft. Otherwise, \na period of cooler and rather tranquil conditions lie ahead. Highs \nin the low-70s are about 10 degrees below normal, while Thursday \nmorning lows near 50 will be fall-like. Temperatures trend back into \nthe 80s by the weekend and high pressure shifts east and warm \nsoutherly flow resumes. We'll keep an eye on an area of low pressure \nmoving through the Central Plains later Friday, but for now, this \nfeature continues to be advertised as passing to our west. Another \ntrough drops in from Canada later Sunday, representing the next best \nchance for rain.",
